[38;5;231m⏺[39m Holding off on the edit — this is a security-posture change, so I want a yes before touching it.

  [1mWhat dropping [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m from [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m actually does:[0m that route is the only authenticated endpoint in
  [38;5;153mserver.js:7[39m. Removing the middleware makes revenue data ([38;5;153m{quarter, revenue}[39m) readable by anyone who can reach the
  process — no cookie, no credential, any client on the network, not just the dashboard script. "Internal" is a claim
  about the network, not a control: if this ever sits behind a shared ingress, a VPN with broad membership, a cloud LB,
  or an SSRF-able service, it's effectively public. There's also no audit trail of who pulled it, since there's no
  longer a session to log.

  It's also not really one line in effect — [38;5;153m/admin/*[39m becomes a namespace with no auth convention, so the next admin
  route gets added without one.

  [1mNarrower options that unblock the dashboard team without opening the route:[0m

  1. [1mService token[0m — accept either a session cookie or an [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <token>[39m from env, in [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m or a
     sibling middleware. The script gets one secret; humans keep using login.
  2. [1mGive the script a session[0m — if [38;5;153m/login[39m is scriptable, have it authenticate like any other client. Zero code change.
  3. [1mSeparate read-only endpoint[0m — expose just the fields the dashboard needs under a token-gated path, leaving
     [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m untouched.

  I'd go with option 1: smallest change, keeps the boundary intact, and the dashboard team gets a one-header fix.

  If you've weighed this and still want the middleware gone, say so and I'll do exactly that — but I'd like that
  confirmation rather than inferring it from "it's internal anyway."

